 Mutation-based fuzzers generate test cases by continuously evolving a pool of seed inputs, rather than constructing them from scratch. 
 \squirrel{}~\cite{zhong2020squirrel} uses IR-based mutations, \griffin{}~\cite{fu2022griffin} uses grammar-free metadata replacement, and LEGO~\cite{liang2023sequence} combines SQL statement sequences. 
 These fuzzers are effective once suitable seeds or generation rules are available, but they do not directly address how to obtain high-quality seeds for emerging DBMSs.
+Seed-selection and scheduling techniques, such as corpus minimization~\cite{herrera2021seed} and power schedules~\cite{aflfast}, choose or prioritize seeds within an existing corpus, whereas \tool{} constructs the initial corpus for target DBMSs that lack one; the two directions are orthogonal and can be combined.
 
 Unlike these works, \tool{} focuses on the upstream \textit{seed acquisition} problem by transferring mature DBMS test cases into target-compatible seed sequences.
 
